  <h1>A website Joe and Suzy made together</h1>
  <p><a href="/joe/creations/">Joseph Neyer Creations</a> was Joe’s design-and-build portfolio: homes, renovations, a drumming studio, a treehouse, and the details that made a space someone’s own.</p>
  <p>This restoration follows the original black-and-gold design, its JNC mark, handwritten lettering, oval photographs, and the way moving between thumbnails brings a different photograph into view. Its artwork and photographs come from the original files Suzy preserved. On a phone, the same projects form a smaller, touch-friendly gallery.</p>
  <p>The original used Adobe Flash. This version uses SVG artwork, HTML links, and a small amount of JavaScript, so it works in current browsers without Flash. Keyboard focus and touch work alongside the original hover behavior. The old phone number and “Email Me” lettering remain part of the historical artwork; they are not current contact links.</p>
  <h2>What survives</h2>
  <p>The shared files contain the homepage and seven full project-gallery screens. The Beery renovation screen survives even though the preserved homepage does not link to it; it is available <a href="/joe/creations/brreno/">here</a>. The porch link on the homepage points to files absent from the shared folder. Its surviving photograph is preserved on <a href="/joe/creations/spporch/">the porch page</a>.</p>
  <p>Short Flash loading animations have been replaced with direct image loading. The original photographs and composition are retained; this is a reconstruction of the portfolio, not a claim that every missing file has been recovered.</p>
